    <string name="date_format_guide"><![CDATA[
            Customize how the date appears on your widget
            <br>
            <br>
            Supported date codes:
            <p>- <b>d</b> = day number (1, 2, 3…)</p>
            <p>- <b>dd</b> = day with zero (01, 02, 03…)</p>
            <p>- <b>M</b> = month number (1, 2, 3…)</p>
            <p>- <b>MM</b> = month with zero (01, 02, 03…)</p>
            <p>- <b>MMM</b> = short month name (Muh, Saf, Rab…)</p>
            <p>- <b>MMMM</b> = full month name (Muharram, Safar…)</p>
            <p>- <b>yy</b> = short year (47, 48…)</p>
            <p>- <b>yyyy</b> = full year (1447, 1448…)</p>
            <p>- <b>EE</b> = short week day (Thu, Fri…)</p>
            <p>- <b>EEEE</b> = full week day (Thursday, Friday…)</p>
            <br>
            Examples:
            <p>- <b>dd/MM/yyyy</b> = 21/02/1447</p>
            <p>- <b>MMMM d, yyyy</b> = Safar 21, 1447</p>
            <p>- <b>EE, MMMM d, yyyy</b> = Fri, Safar 21, 1447</p>
            <p>- <b>d-M-yy</b> = 21-2-47</p>
            <br>
            Mix and match these codes with slashes, dashes, spaces, or commas to create your preferred date style.
            <br>
            <br>
            <p>Literal text: put text in single quotes so it is not interpreted as date codes or language groups.</p>
            <p>- <b>d &#39;days&#39;</b> = 21 days</p>
            <p>- <b>&#39;en-GB{hello}&#39;</b> = en-GB{hello}</p>
            <p>Use two single quotes (<b>&#39;&#39;</b>) to display one apostrophe.</p>
            <br>
            By default the language used for displaying these codes is the device language but you can specify a different language by using this syntax:
            <br>
            <br>
                 &#x2000;&#x2000;&#x2000;&#x2000;<big>&lt;language-code&gt;{date-code}</big>
            <br>
            <br>
            Language tags can include just a language (<b>ar</b>), a region (<b>en-GB</b>), or a script and region (<b>zh-Hant-TW</b>).
            <p>- <b>ar{MMMM}</b> displays the month in Arabic.</p>
            <p>- <b>zh-Hant-TW{MMMM}</b> displays the month in Traditional Chinese.</p>
            <br>
            Examples:
            <p>- <b>en-GB{dd/MMMM/yyyy}</b>= 21/Safar/1447</p>
            <p>- <b>ar-SA{dd MMMM yyyy}</b>= &#x200F;۲۱ صفر ۱٤٤۷</p>
            <p>- <b>hi-IN{dd MMMM yyyy}</b>= 06 सफर 1447</p>
            <p>- <b>tr-TR{dd MMMM yyyy}</b>= 06 Rebiülevvel 1447</p>
            <br>
            You can also mix and match multiple languages:
            <p>- <b>en-GB{dd}/ar-SA{MMMM}/en-GB{yyyy}</b> = 21/صفر&#x200E;/1447</p>
            <p>- <b>en-GB{dd} ar-SA{MMMM} en-GB{yyyy}</b> = 21 صفر&#x200E; 1447</p>
            <br>
            <p>Device-language groups: use <b>{...}</b> without a language tag to keep a component in the device language.</p>
            <p>- <b>{d} ar{MMMM} {yyyy}</b> uses device-language numbers and an Arabic month.</p>
            <p>Groups also keep adjacent components separate: <b>{d}{d}</b> displays the day twice, while <b>dd</b> displays one day padded to two digits.</p>
            <br>
            For the full list of language identifiers:
            <br>
            <a href="%1$s">%1$s</a>
            ]]>
    </string>
